\description{
\code{netis_data()} builds the single data structure that both \code{\link[=fit_influence]{fit_influence()}}
and \code{\link[=fit_selection]{fit_selection()}} consume. It holds node, tie, and (optionally) group data at
their natural grain -- nodes, ties, and groups are never flattened into one
duplicated table -- and it never renames a node characteristic to "ego" or
"alter": which role a variable plays is decided by model terms, not by storage.
}
\section{Static and dynamic variables}{
If \code{wave_col} is supplied, a variable is treated as \emph{dynamic} when its value
actually changes across waves within an entity, and \emph{static} when it does not.
This classification only affects internal storage (static values are held once
per entity rather than duplicated at every wave); it never affects what
\code{\link[=netis_nodes]{netis_nodes()}}, \code{\link[=netis_ties]{netis_ties()}}, or \code{\link[=netis_groups]{netis_groups()}} return, which is always the
full logical long-format table.
}

\section{Wave order}{
The order of the waves is read from the labels' type, once, when the object
is built:
\itemize{
\item numbers and dates run in increasing order;
\item a factor runs in the order of its levels, and factor columns in several
tables must agree on that order;
\item text that reads as numbers (\code{"9"}, \code{"10"}) runs by numeric value, whether or
not another table stores the same waves as numbers;
\item text that does not read as numbers has no order rnetis can know, so two or
more such labels are refused: supply them as a factor whose levels give the
time order.
}

Missing wave labels are refused. Every lag and history computation steps
through this order by position, never by subtracting labels, so labels 1, 3, 5
are three consecutive occasions and no spacing is implied.

The waves are those found in any table. A table with no wave column holds at
every wave. A table with a wave column that covers only some of the waves is
not reused at the others: a tie table observed at wave 1 only describes the
network at wave 1, and the network at the other waves is unobserved.
}

\section{Empty and unobserved waves}{
A group's network at a wave is in one of three states when ties carry a wave
column:
\itemize{
\item \strong{observed, with ties}: the group has tie rows at that wave;
\item \strong{observed and empty}: the group has no tie rows at that wave, and the
wave is recorded as observed. An all-zero matrix given to
\code{\link[=netis_data_from_adjacency_series]{netis_data_from_adjacency_series()}} and an edgeless graph given to
\code{\link[=netis_data_from_igraph_list]{netis_data_from_igraph_list()}} are recorded this way, and for a tie table
\code{observed_tie_waves} says so. Every node there is an isolate, and exposure
follows the isolate rule;
\item \strong{unobserved}: the group has no tie rows at that wave, is not recorded as
observed there, and has an observed network at another wave. Exposure and
network features there are \code{NA} with a warning, a Selection fit treats the
dyads as missing, and the exports warn and return no ties.
}

A group with no observed network at any wave has established nothing either
way, so its nodes are treated as isolates and \code{\link[=diagnose_data]{diagnose_data()}} notes it. The
record of observed networks is \code{netis_schema(x)$observed_tie_waves}.
}

\section{Undirected data}{
Undirected tie data stores \strong{one row per unordered pair} (per group, and per
wave when ties carry one), with the endpoints in either column order. Storing
both \code{a -> b} and \code{b -> a} is an error. Every network rnetis builds from
undirected data -- exposure, network features, adjacency export, graph export,
\code{latent()} -- reads that one row as a tie in both directions, with its stored
value (negative values included) on both. \code{mode} and the two \code{direction}
readings therefore make no difference on undirected data.
}

\section{The node roster}{
A node is a (group, node) pair: the same label in two groups is two nodes. A
group's \strong{roster} is every node with a row for that group in \code{node_data} at
any wave, and a node table with no wave column puts every node on the roster
at every wave.

A tie naming a node that is not on its group's roster is accepted here and
refused by every function that builds a network from it, because it cannot be
placed; \code{\link[=diagnose_data]{diagnose_data()}} lists all of them. A node on the roster with no ties
is an isolate.

A node on the roster may have no row at a particular wave. What then happens
depends on what is being built:
\itemize{
\item \code{\link[=compute_exposure]{compute_exposure()}}, \code{\link[=compute_centrality]{compute_centrality()}} and \code{\link[=compute_network_stat]{compute_network_stat()}}
report one row per node with a row at that wave. A tie at that wave that
reaches a rostered node without a row is still a tie: the node stays in the
network as a vertex, its alters' degree and strength count it, its content
is missing, and an exposure that depends on that content is \code{NA}.
\item \code{\link[=netis_adjacency]{netis_adjacency()}} keeps the whole roster at every wave, so that matrices
from two waves of a group are conformable.
\item \code{\link[=as_igraph]{as_igraph()}} and \code{\link[=as_network]{as_network()}} use the node rows at the selected wave as
the vertex set, so a tie reaching a rostered node without a row there is an
error.
}
}

\section{Direction}{
\code{netis_data()} preserves tie direction exactly as supplied: \code{source_col} and
\code{target_col} are never swapped or reinterpreted here. Converting to the
canonical exposure-mathematics convention (\code{W[i,j]} = the strength with which
node \code{j} influences node \code{i}) is the exposure engine's job, not this
constructor's -- see \code{\link[=compute_exposure]{compute_exposure()}}'s Direction section.
}

\section{What is and is not validated here}{
This constructor checks that the object is \emph{structurally} well formed: required
columns are present, no identifier is missing (a group, node, source or target
that is \code{NA} is refused), and no entity key (node, tie, or group -- crossed
with wave if supplied) is duplicated -- including, for undirected data, the same
unordered pair stored in both orientations. It does \strong{not} check that tie
endpoints are on the roster (see The node roster), check the declared
\code{self_ties} policy (every function that builds a network refuses a nonzero
self-tie under \code{"prohibited"}), or flag isolates, missing waves, or other
design-level concerns. Those belong to \code{\link[=diagnose_data]{diagnose_data()}} and
\code{\link[=diagnose_design]{diagnose_design()}}, run separately, because they are about whether an
otherwise well-formed dataset supports the analysis a user is attempting --
not about whether the object can be constructed at all.
}

\section{Errors and refusals}{
\code{netis_data()} itself refuses only on the shape of what it was handed:
\code{node_data} and \code{tie_data} must be data frames, \code{group_data} a data frame or
\code{NULL}, and \code{tie_meta} / \code{derived_meta} named lists keyed by variable name or
\code{NULL}. Each names the offending argument.

It also refuses what would make the object's keys or declarations unreadable:
a missing or duplicated key, a wave column it cannot order, a \code{tie_meta} entry
that does not fit (see \code{tie_meta}), and a \code{time_meta} that contradicts the wave
order.

\strong{Undetermined edge presence is recorded here, not refused here}, and the
distinction is deliberate. A \code{complete_dyadic} network with neither
\code{presence_col} nor \code{presence_rule} is a perfectly constructible object: the
rows are observations of dyads, and nothing is wrong until something asks
which dyads are \emph{tied}. The refusal therefore belongs to the operations that
need presence — \code{\link[=as_network]{as_network()}}, \code{\link[=as_igraph]{as_igraph()}}, \code{binarize(rule = presence())},
the measures computed from presence such as degree and density, the network
plots, and adjacency extraction with \code{presence = TRUE} — each of which errors
naming the tie variable and the ways to resolve it (declare \code{presence_col} or
\code{presence_rule} here, or declare a \code{presence_mode} that fits the data).
Refusing at construction instead would reject data that is entirely usable
for every analysis that never asks about presence.
}

